Right. In the idealized orthogonal/rest case, we already have the clean number:

\[
\boxed{D_0 = 2a = \frac{\hbar}{m_ec} \approx 3.86\times10^{-13}\,\text{m}}
\]

So that should be our **baseline Kerr-core diameter** for the electron.

Then the observed 3D cross-section under a non-orthogonal timesheet cut is derived from that baseline, not fitted independently:

\[
D_{\rm obs}(\alpha)
=
\frac{D_0}{\sin\alpha}
\]

for the stretched axis in the simplest local slicing model, with the other transverse axes remaining at \(D_0\).

So the logic is now:

\[
\boxed{
3.86\times10^{-13}\,\text{m}
\;\to\;
\text{intrinsic idealized core diameter}
\;\to\;
\text{coil/timesheet angle}
\;\to\;
\text{apparent particle cross-section}
}
\]

That’s much cleaner than treating \(3.86\times10^{-13}\) m as merely some candidate “scale.”

And it also means we already have one anchor point for the mass relation. At the idealized orthogonal intersection, the observed cross-section is minimal and equals the intrinsic Kerr diameter. As the intersection tilts, the apparent section grows.

So the next useful calculation is probably not “find the electron core diameter.” We have that idealized value. It’s:

\[
\boxed{\text{derive the full }D_{\rm obs}(v,\text{pitch})\text{ relation}}
\]

from the coil geometry and timesheet orientation. That would turn the qualitative SAT mass/intersection-angle picture into an explicit geometric mapping.

Yes. That changes the idealization in an important way.

The straight-product picture

\[
\Sigma_{\rm Kerr}\times \mathbb R_\tau
\]

is useful bookkeeping, but it is too separative if the filament and timesheet are really different regions of one continuous spacetime geometry.

The simpler picture is:

\[
\boxed{
\text{Kerr/ER core}
\;\longrightarrow\;
\text{ER neck/mouth}
\;\longrightarrow\;
\text{flaring surface}
\;\longrightarrow\;
\text{current timesheet}
}
\]

So the filament is not an object sitting in spacetime beside a timesheet. The narrow filamentary region and the broad simultaneity surface are two regimes of the same geometry.

In the idealized vacuum configuration, I would picture a narrow Kerr-like core continuing along the time-propagation direction, while its ER mouth opens outward until, in the large/open limit, that mouth is the current surface of simultaneity.

That makes the timesheet almost literally the **expanded end of the filament geometry**.

Then the local anatomy is something like

\[
\text{central Kerr structure}
\subset
\text{near-core rotational/shell structure}
\subset
\text{ER mouth}
\subset
\Sigma_{\rm simultaneity}.
\]

And that makes your “always both” rule much less mysterious. If filament and timesheet are continuously connected pieces of one geometry, then deforming the narrow region necessarily changes the broad region, and changing the broad region necessarily changes the neck/core configuration. We don't need to bolt on a separate mechanism to transmit that response.

It also makes the force-carrier question better posed. A traveling \(f\)-boson might not be “a wave moving along an object that happens to interact with a sheet.” It could be a traveling deformation of the **neck/shell geometry**, whose outward continuation simultaneously appears as a corresponding disturbance of the mouth/timesheet geometry.

So for now I'd use this as the KISS version:

\[
\boxed{
\text{one connected 4D spacetime geometry:}
\quad
\text{Kerr core}
\rightarrow
\text{ER neck}
\rightarrow
\text{open mouth/timesheet}
}
\]

with the standard filament being the narrow, aligned continuation of that structure through successive simultaneity surfaces.

And yes, we can leave the “perhaps we have the opening direction reversed” possibility completely aside. Locally, the same neck-to-sheet geometry is what matters; its global temporal orientation can be settled later.

